    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is DocuSign better than Dropbox Sign for businesses?</h3>
    <p>DocuSign is better for large businesses and enterprises that need advanced compliance, complex workflows, bulk sending, and deep integrations with Salesforce and SAP. Dropbox Sign is better for small to mid-size businesses that want a simpler, more affordable e-signature tool with a cleaner interface. For most small businesses sending under 100 documents per month, Dropbox Sign is easier to use. For enterprises with complex signing workflows and compliance requirements, DocuSign is the stronger choice.</p>

    <h3>Which is cheaper, DocuSign or Dropbox Sign?</h3>
    <p>Both platforms start at $15 per month for their entry plans. DocuSign Personal at $15 per month includes 5 documents per month, while Dropbox Sign Essential at $15 per month includes unlimited self-signing and 3 documents per month for requesting signatures. At higher tiers, DocuSign Business Pro at $45 per user per month adds bulk send, conditional fields, and advanced workflow. Dropbox Sign Business at $30 per user per month includes templates, team management, and unlimited templates. For small teams, Dropbox Sign is often slightly cheaper at comparable tiers.</p>

    <h3>Which has better integrations, DocuSign or Dropbox Sign?</h3>
    <p>DocuSign has broader and deeper integrations, especially with enterprise systems like Salesforce, SAP, Workday, Microsoft Dynamics, and Google Workspace. DocuSign also offers over 400 pre-built integrations and a robust API for custom integrations. Dropbox Sign integrates well with Dropbox, Google Workspace, Slack, HubSpot, and Salesforce, with fewer total integrations but a cleaner setup. For enterprise systems, DocuSign wins. For small business tools and Dropbox users, Dropbox Sign integrates more seamlessly.</p>

    <h3>Can I migrate from Dropbox Sign to DocuSign?</h3>
    <p>Yes, you can migrate from Dropbox Sign to DocuSign. You can export completed documents and audit trails from Dropbox Sign as PDFs with certification, then upload them to DocuSign for archival. Templates and signing workflows need to be rebuilt in DocuSign because the two platforms use different template structures. Most businesses migrate when they outgrow Dropbox Sign's feature set, typically when they need bulk sending, conditional routing, or enterprise compliance that Dropbox Sign does not offer.</p>

    <h3>Which is more secure, DocuSign or Dropbox Sign?</h3>
    <p>Both platforms are highly secure and compliant with major standards including SOC 2 Type II, ESIGN Act, eIDAS, and GDPR. DocuSign has a slight edge for enterprise security with additional certifications like FedRAMP Moderate authorization, ISO 27001, and HIPAA compliance on higher tiers. DocuSign also offers more granular access controls, document retention policies, and advanced authentication options like SMS, knowledge-based authentication, and ID verification. Dropbox Sign is secure for most business use cases but has fewer enterprise-grade compliance options.</p>

    <h3>Can I use DocuSign and Dropbox Sign together?</h3>
    <p>It is not practical to use both platforms for the same documents, but some organizations use DocuSign for high-value contracts and compliance documents while using Dropbox Sign for routine internal approvals and NDAs. A more common approach is to pick one platform based on your document volume and complexity. If you send under 100 documents per month and need simple workflows, Dropbox Sign is sufficient. If you need bulk sending, conditional routing, or enterprise compliance, DocuSign is the better choice. Most businesses consolidate on one platform within the first few months.</p>

